WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.824 --> 00:00:13.480
Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra is the author of one of the world's most famous novels, Don Quixote de la Mancha.

2
00:00:13.481 --> 00:00:23.203
This is the story of a country gentleman who decides to become a knight after reading many romantic tales about knights in armor and highborn ladies.

3
00:00:23.204 --> 00:00:34.127
This gentleman takes the name Don Quixote, and he and his down-to-earth squire, Sancho Panza, go on quests to find adventure and fight injustice.

4
00:00:34.128 --> 00:00:37.576
This simple story changed literature forever.

5
00:00:37.577 --> 00:00:47.394
Cervantes was born in 1547 outside of Madrid, Spain, in the city of Alcalá de Henares. He was one of seven children.

6
00:00:47.395 --> 00:00:53.894
Not much is known of his early childhood, but one thing is certain—he was an eager reader of books.

7
00:00:53.895 --> 00:00:59.901
In the fall of 1569, young Cervantes went to Rome to work for the Catholic Church.

8
00:00:59.902 --> 00:01:06.113
Some claim he really went to Rome to escape arrest for having wounded another man in a duel.

9
00:01:06.114 --> 00:01:19.556
Whatever the reason, being in Rome allowed him to study art, philosophy, and literature and to experience the many changes happening in society during the transition from medieval times to the early Renaissance.

10
00:01:19.557 --> 00:01:24.753
Scientific thinking was on the rise and traditional knowledge was being challenged.

11
00:01:24.754 --> 00:01:32.142
Cosmopolitan areas, where people with different beliefs and experiences tended to come together, were expanding.

12
00:01:32.143 --> 00:01:37.409
In 1570, when he was 24, Cervantes joined the Spanish army.

13
00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:47.904
At that time, the Muslim Turks of the Ottoman Empire were fighting European Christians for control of the Mediterranean, and Cervantes wanted to be a part of the fight.

14
00:01:47.905 --> 00:01:59.677
He joined the Holy League, a mighty armada of three hundred ships and the Catholic armies of Pope Pius V, Venice, and Spain, which totaled about eighty thousand men.

15
00:01:59.678 --> 00:02:07.503
In 1571, Cervantes was on a ship near Greece when he was shot three times in the Battle of Lepanto.

16
00:02:07.504 --> 00:02:14.109
One bullet hit him in the left hand, crippling it. He was never able to use that hand again.

17
00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:23.101
The battle was a huge victory for the Holy League, and Cervantes later wrote, "On that day, Ottoman pride and arrogance were broken."

18
00:02:23.102 --> 00:02:29.718
Cervantes served for four more years, fighting in several more military campaigns against the Turks.

19
00:02:29.719 --> 00:02:39.911
He was promoted and was now receiving a high salary; nevertheless, tired of war, Cervantes decided to return to Spain in 1575.

20
00:02:39.912 --> 00:02:44.869
That September, he boarded a galley named El Sol in Naples, Italy.

21
00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:53.187
The ship was attacked by Turkish pirates, and Cervantes and the other survivors of the attack were taken to Algiers as captives.

22
00:02:53.188 --> 00:03:03.726
A city on the coast of North Africa, Algiers was the center of Turkish piracy, where supplies, stolen merchandise, and slaves were freely bought and sold.

23
00:03:03.727 --> 00:03:12.383
Prisoners were treated like slaves, forced to work all day and night with little food, water, or rest. Many died in captivity.

24
00:03:12.384 --> 00:03:17.661
If a slave was wealthy, pirates would attempt to get a ransom from the person's family.

25
00:03:17.662 --> 00:03:26.535
Because Cervantes had letters of recommendation from important people in the Spanish army, the Turks thought he must be a valuable captive.

26
00:03:26.536 --> 00:03:31.591
Even though Cervantes told them he had no money, the Turks did not believe him.

27
00:03:31.592 --> 00:03:38.591
They decided to ransom him for five hundred gold coins, a sum his family could hardly hope to raise.

28
00:03:38.592 --> 00:03:43.421
Cervantes made four escape attempts but was captured every time.

29
00:03:43.422 --> 00:03:49.643
After five years in captivity, Cervantes' ransom was finally paid by a group of friars.

30
00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:58.747
Cervantes returned home to Spain in 1580, but his long imprisonment had left an enduring mark on him as a man and as a writer.

31
00:03:58.748 --> 00:04:03.595
As soon as he reached home, Cervantes started his writing career in earnest.

32
00:04:03.596 --> 00:04:10.968
In 1585, a few months after getting married, Cervantes published the romantic novel La Galatea.

33
00:04:10.969 --> 00:04:19.627
Some of his plays were being performed in theaters in Madrid as well, but they did not provide Cervantes with much money to take care of his household.

34
00:04:19.628 --> 00:04:32.591
So, in 1587, Cervantes accepted a government position requisitioning grain and other supplies for the Spanish Armada, a fleet of over one hundred ships being prepared to battle England.

35
00:04:32.592 --> 00:04:47.087
After the armada was destroyed in 1588 by Elizabeth I and her navy—a horrible embarrassment for Spain—Cervantes became a tax collector, going from town to town and collecting rents and taxes for the government.

36
00:04:47.088 --> 00:04:58.075
Suspicion that he was falsifying his accounts got him in trouble with the government, and he wound up in jail twice over the course of six years for financial impropriety.

37
00:04:58.076 --> 00:05:03.742
Many scholars, however, believe that he was honest but simply a bad businessman.

38
00:05:03.743 --> 00:05:09.623
Cervantes later said that part of his masterpiece Don Quixote was written in prison.

39
00:05:09.624 --> 00:05:16.011
In 1605, at the age of 58, Cervantes published the first part of Don Quixote.

40
00:05:16.012 --> 00:05:23.843
The novel was a huge success, one he badly needed after years of intense hardship and financial insecurity.

41
00:05:23.844 --> 00:05:30.009
Ten years later, he published the second part, which some critics think surpasses the first.

42
00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:41.107
Cervantes had lost much of his idealism about the Spanish Empire and the romance of battle, and in Don Quixote, he makes fun of everyone—nobility and commoners.

43
00:05:41.108 --> 00:05:50.101
His main character, Don Quixote, bases his life's purpose on upholding the "truth" that he has read about in romantic stories.

44
00:05:50.102 --> 00:05:59.086
Don Quixote does battle with windmills, thinking they are giants, and mistakes a farm girl for a wellborn lady needing his protection.

45
00:05:59.087 --> 00:06:04.411
When he comes to a small inn, the knight thinks it is the castle of a great lord.

46
00:06:04.412 --> 00:06:14.349
Sancho Panza constantly tries to get his master to see things as they really are, but Don Quixote insists on seeing the world in magical ways.

47
00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:21.515
Readers everywhere were touched by both the humor and the sadness of Cervantes' depiction of the human experience.

48
00:06:21.516 --> 00:06:26.853
The story is a comedy that makes fun of idealistic and impractical people.

49
00:06:26.854 --> 00:06:33.671
But at the same time, it is a tragedy about a troubled man struggling with self-delusion and social change.

50
00:06:33.672 --> 00:06:39.613
It remains up to the reader to decide whether Don Quixote is a hero or a fool.

51
00:06:39.614 --> 00:06:47.209
Cervantes wrote many poems, novels, short stories, and plays, but none became as famous as Don Quixote.

52
00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:53.436
The book about the wayward knight is thought by many scholars to be the basis of the modern novel.

53
00:06:53.437 --> 00:06:58.984
Before Cervantes, most stories were written to convey unchanging moral "truths."

54
00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:11.145
Cervantes broke that tradition and created a completely new type of literature, which questions what is real and what is not through characters who are themselves undecided about reality.

55
00:07:11.146 --> 00:07:17.463
Not only did Cervantes help invent a new kind of story, but he also inspired a new word.

56
00:07:17.464 --> 00:07:25.800
We use this word today to sum up a person or plan that is idealistic, impulsive, and romantic: quixotic.
